¢¨¯F—zÚ¢ƒüTÞˆò+Nv™¥që+¯¸n{"rendered":{"html":"<p>Some values matter deep inside a program without every function in\nbetween caring about them: the platform you are building for, for\nexample, or the toolchains available. A <em>context</em> is a named value of\nthat kind. You declare it once, read it with <code>^</code>, and set it for a call\nand everything that call does with <code>with</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\" data-language=\"gcsl\"><button class=\"copy-code\" type=\"button\" data-copy-code><span class=\"copy-label\">Copy</span></button><pre><code>context arch: String = \"x86_64\"\n\nfn compiler(): String { \"gcc-{^arch}\" }\nfn build(): String { \"built with {compiler()}\" }\n\nnative = build()\ncross = build() with { arch = \"aarch64\" }\n</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>build</code> never mentions <code>arch</code>, yet <code>cross</code> is built with <code>gcc-aarch64</code>:\nthe <code>with</code> reaches every call made inside it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"declaring-a-context\">Declaring a context <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#declaring-a-context\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p><code>context NAME: TYPE = DEFAULT</code> declares a context and its default, at the\ntop level of a file. Every file of the workspace can read it without an\nimport, so a name is declared only once. A context can leave out its\ndefault, <code>context toolchains: List&lt;Toolchain&gt;</code>; reading it then needs a\n<code>with</code> that sets it, and reading it without one is an error.</p>\n<p>A default is an expression, and it is evaluated where the context is\nread, in the context of the read. So one context can default to another,\nand follow it:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\" data-language=\"gcsl\"><button class=\"copy-code\" type=\"button\" data-copy-code><span class=\"copy-label\">Copy</span></button><pre><code>type Platform = {arch: String, os: String}\ncontext host_platform: Platform = {arch = \"x86_64\"; os = \"linux\"}\ncontext platform: Platform = ^host_platform\n\ntarget = ^platform.arch with { host_platform = {arch = \"aarch64\"; os = \"linux\"} }\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>A default must be pure: it can't call an impure function. To use a value\nthat needs one, compute it first and bind it with <code>with</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"setting-a-context-with-with\">Setting a context with <code>with</code> <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#setting-a-context-with-with\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p><code>EXPR with { NAME = VALUE }</code> evaluates <code>EXPR</code> with the context <code>NAME</code>\nset to <code>VALUE</code>. It applies to the call or field access just before it,\nso <code>a + f() with {...}</code> sets the context for <code>f()</code> alone. It must be on\nthe line where the expression ends. To set a context for several\nstatements, use the block form, <code>with { NAME = VALUE } { ... }</code>.</p>\n<p>A dotted name sets one field of a record context and keeps the others:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\" data-language=\"gcsl\"><button class=\"copy-code\" type=\"button\" data-copy-code><span class=\"copy-label\">Copy</span></button><pre><code>type Platform = {arch: String, os: String}\ncontext platform: Platform = {arch = \"x86_64\"; os = \"linux\"}\n\nfn triple(): String { \"{^platform.arch}-{^platform.os}\" }\nriscv = triple() with { platform.arch = \"riscv64\" }\n</code></pre></div>\n<p><code>with { arch }</code> is short for <code>with { arch = arch }</code>, setting the context\nfrom a variable of the same name.</p>\n<h2 id=\"reading-a-context-in-a-parameter\">Reading a context in a parameter <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#reading-a-context-in-a-parameter\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>A parameter written <code>^NAME</code> takes its default from the context of that\nname, so a caller can still pass a value explicitly. <code>fn f(^arch)</code> is the\nsame as <code>fn f(arch: String = ^arch)</code>, with the type taken from the\ncontext's declaration:</p>\n<div class=\"code-block\" data-language=\"gcsl\"><button class=\"copy-code\" type=\"button\" data-copy-code><span class=\"copy-label\">Copy</span></button><pre><code>context arch: String = \"x86_64\"\nfn compiler(^arch): String { \"gcc-{arch}\" }\n\nusual = compiler()\nexplicit = compiler(arch = \"mips\")\n</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"context-and-deferred-work\">Context and deferred work <a class=\"heading-anchor\" href=\"#context-and-deferred-work\" aria-label=\"Link to this section\">#</a></h2>\n<p>A <code>lazy</code> keeps the context it was made in. Wherever it is later\nawaited, it reads the values that were set where it was written. A\ntop-level field is computed when its file runs, so it reads the default\nvalues.</p>\n<p>Memoised functions stay correct: a memoised call's result is reused only\nfor a call made in a context that gives every context the call read the\nsame value.</p>\n","toc":[{"level":2,"id":"declaring-a-context","title":"Declaring a context","number":null},{"level":2,"id":"setting-a-context-with-with","title":"Setting a context with with","number":null},{"level":2,"id":"reading-a-context-in-a-parameter","title":"Reading a context in a parameter","number":null},{"level":2,"id":"context-and-deferred-work","title":"Context and deferred work","number":null}],"has_mermaid":false,"dangling":[],"warnings":[],"broken":[]},"images":[],"links":{"pages":[],"anchors":[]},"refs":[]}